.TH CMEMPOOL 7 "2026-10-14" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Overview"
.SH NAME
cmempool \- overview of the fixed-size and ranged memory pools
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cmempool.h>
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
cmempool has two kinds of memory pool:
  - A ccol_mempool holds a fixed number of entries of one size and
    hands them out and takes them back in O(1) time through a free
    list. ccol_mempool_create(3) takes its block from an allocator,
    and ccol_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer(3) builds it in a
    buffer that the caller owns, which you declare with the right size
    through CCOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_MEMPOOL_BUFFER(3).
  - A ccol_r_mempool, the ranged pool, holds one fixed-size pool (a
    tier) for each power of two between a smallest and a largest size,
    and serves each request from the smallest tier that fits it. Each
    tier holds half the entries of the tier below it.
    ccol_r_mempool_create(3),
    ccol_r_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer(3) and
    CCOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_RMEMPOOL_BUFFER(3) create one, and
    ccol_r_mempool_realloc_entry(3) resizes an entry.
.fi
.SS "Entries"
.nf
Every entry a pool hands out, a fallback entry included, is aligned
for any object type, which is the same guarantee that malloc(3) gives.
The stride between entries is the element size rounded up to 16 bytes,
and then up to a power of two unless the library was built with
CCOL_MEMPOOL_COMPACT_LAYOUT=1. A new entry is uninitialized, except
when it comes from one of the calloc forms.

An entry goes back to the pool it came from, and the free call names
that pool. The pool decides from the address alone whether the entry
is its own before it reads anything through it, so an entry of another
pool, an address the pool never handed out and a double free all end
in an assertion that stops the process.
.fi
.SS "Running out of entries"
.nf
A ccol_mempool created with fallback_to_dynamic_memory allocates from
its allocator when it has no free entry left, so an allocation fails
only when the system is out of memory. Without the fallback an empty
pool returns NULL. A ranged pool takes a fallback policy instead.
With ccol_fallback_disabled it tries each larger tier and then returns
NULL. With ccol_fallback_at_first_exhaustion it goes to the heap as
soon as the tier that fits the request is empty, and with
ccol_fallback_at_last_exhaustion it tries every larger tier first and
goes to the heap only after all of them are empty.
.fi
.SS "Ownership and lifetime"
.nf
ccol_mempool_destroy(3) and ccol_r_mempool_destroy(3) free the pool
and set the handle to NULL. Every fallback entry must be freed before
the destroy, or the destroy asserts. For a pool built in a
preallocated buffer the destroy frees only the pool structure, and the
buffer stays the caller's.

A pool calls its allocator for its fallback entries and for its own
bookkeeping of them while it holds its own lock (when it has one), so
an allocator that is built on a pool must draw from a different pool.
A pool that threads share can call the free function of its allocator
after its destroy returns, even after main() returns, because each
thread's cache is freed later by that thread; ccol_mempool_destroy(3)
has the details. A pool created with single_threaded has no such tail.
.fi
.SS "Capacity"
.nf
ccol_mempool_total_capacity(3) reports how many entries the pool
promises, and ccol_mempool_used_count(3) how many callers hold right
now. A thread-safe pool of 8 entries or more keeps a small cache of
entries for each thread, and allocates a reserve above its count so
that the promised count stays available to every thread. An entry
leaves a thread's cache with no lock held, so concurrent callers can
hold a few entries more than the promised count for a short time, but
never more than that count plus the reserve. All of that memory is
allocated when the pool is created and nothing grows at run time.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
A translation unit that declares a buffer with
CCOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_MEMPOOL_BUFFER(3) must be built with the same
CCOL_MEMPOOL_COMPACT_LAYOUT setting as the library. If the two
disagree, the link fails with an error that names the expected setting.

Which sizes, tier count and single_threaded setting work best depends
on how the program allocates; ccol_mempool_create(3) and
ccol_r_mempool_create(3) explain the trade-offs.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
A pool created with single_threaded set has no lock, and only one
thread at a time may use it. Any other pool can be used by many
threads at once. A pool that keeps a cache for each thread takes no
lock on its common allocate and free path, and an entry that one
thread allocated can be freed by another one. Each tier of a ranged
pool has its own lock. Two threads must not free the same entry, and
no thread may use a pool while another thread destroys it.
.fi
